  summary: `# 🧬 Rec Adv Bioscience #2.1 — ELISA + Lateral Flow + HA/HI

## 🎯 ELISA (Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay)

### Principle
- Antibody-antigen binding + enzyme color reaction
- Quantitative or semi-quantitative
- Most common immunoassay in vet diagnostics

### Formats

#### Direct ELISA
- Antigen → enzyme-conjugated antibody → substrate
- Simple, fewer steps
- Lower sensitivity

#### Indirect ELISA (most common)
- Antigen → primary Ab → enzyme-conjugated secondary Ab → substrate
- Higher sensitivity (signal amplification)
- For antibody detection in serum

#### Sandwich ELISA
- Capture Ab → Antigen → detection Ab → enzyme → substrate
- For antigen detection
- Two-Ab specificity (high specificity)

#### Competitive ELISA
- Sample antigen competes with labeled antigen for Ab binding
- Inverse signal: more sample = less signal
- Useful for small molecules, hormones

### Common substrates
- TMB (3,3',5,5'-Tetramethylbenzidine) → blue
- ABTS → green
- OPD → orange
- Stop with H2SO4 → yellow (TMB)

### Reading + interpretation
- OD measured at 450 nm (TMB)
- Cutoff: mean negative + 2-3 SD
- S/P ratio (sample to positive ratio)
- Standard curve for quantitative

### Vet applications
- PRRS, PCV2, Mhp, APP, Brucella, Lepto, Bovine TB serology
- Pet vaccine titers (rabies, parvo)
- Aflatoxin in feed
- Hormone (cortisol, progesterone)

## 🎯 Lateral Flow Immunoassay (Rapid Test)

### Principle
- Capillary action moves sample across membrane
- Antibody-antigen capture at test line
- Visual color readout (no equipment)
- 5-15 minutes

### Components
- Sample pad
- Conjugate pad (Ab + colored particle, e.g., gold or latex)
- Test line (capture Ab)
- Control line (anti-conjugate Ab)
- Absorbent pad

### Vet applications
- IDEXX SNAP tests (heartworm, parvo, FeLV, FIV)
- Rapid AI test (poultry)
- Pregnancy test (cattle, equine)
- ASF rapid test
- Brucella, FMD field test

### Strengths + limitations
- Strengths: fast, point-of-care, no equipment, easy interpretation
- Limitations: lower sensitivity than ELISA, qualitative only, not for low-abundance

## 🐔 Hemagglutination (HA) test

### Principle
- Some viruses (influenza, NDV) bind red blood cells (RBCs) via hemagglutinin
- Causes RBC agglutination (lattice formation)
- Detect virus presence + titer

### Technique
- 2-fold serial dilution of virus + standardized RBC suspension
- Read after 30-45 min at room temp
- Endpoint: highest dilution showing agglutination
- HA titer = reciprocal of endpoint

## 🐔 Hemagglutination Inhibition (HI) test

### Principle
- Patient serum (containing Ab) blocks virus from agglutinating RBCs
- Detects ANTIBODY against the virus
- Most common for influenza serotyping + serology

### Technique
- 2-fold serial dilution of serum
- Add 4-8 HA units of virus → incubate
- Add RBCs → incubate
- Read endpoint (no agglutination = inhibition)
- HI titer = reciprocal of last serum dilution showing inhibition

### Application
- Influenza serotyping (H1, H3, H5, H7)
- NDV antibody titer (vaccination monitoring)
- Equine arteritis

## 📊 Test selection by purpose

| Purpose | Test |
|---|---|
| Antigen detection (acute infection) | Sandwich ELISA, Lateral flow, PCR |
| Antibody (exposure history) | Indirect ELISA, IFA |
| Virus titer | HA |
| Serotype identification | HI |
| Field rapid screening | Lateral flow |
| Lab high-throughput | ELISA |

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ Direct/Indirect/Sandwich/Competitive ELISA = 4 formats
2. ⭐ Lateral flow = field-friendly, 5-15 min
3. ⭐ HA test detects virus; HI test detects Ab
4. ⭐ HI titer = reciprocal of last inhibitory dilution
5. ⭐ Sandwich ELISA = antigen detection (high specificity)`,
